1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,319
A history is drawn commit by commit. Here
main has three commits, and feature/login

2
00:00:09,359 --> 00:00:10,607
leaves it at commit B.

3
00:00:12,446 --> 00:00:15,825
On the left and on the right, the
same command is about to run.

4
00:00:16,165 --> 00:00:18,941
On the left, main is an
ancestor of feature.

5
00:00:19,281 --> 00:00:22,621
So the label slides forward.
That is a fast-forward.

6
00:00:22,961 --> 00:00:27,930
On the right the two branches diverged.
Git walks back from both tips to the merge

7
00:00:27,970 --> 00:00:28,236
base.

8
00:00:28,576 --> 00:00:30,842
Then it writes a new
commit with two parents.

9
00:00:32,682 --> 00:00:35,541
A rebase starts from the
same diverged picture.

10
00:00:35,881 --> 00:00:38,609
Git lifts the commits that
exist only on feature.

11
00:00:38,949 --> 00:00:42,383
It copies them, one at a
time, onto the tip of main.

12
00:00:42,423 --> 00:00:45,056
Each copy is a new commit with a new ID.

13
00:00:45,396 --> 00:00:49,039
The originals are still there, but
they fade: nothing points at them.

14
00:00:49,379 --> 00:00:52,182
Finally the branch label
moves to the last copy.

15
00:00:54,022 --> 00:00:58,119
Git keeps three versions of your file:
the working tree, the index, and HEAD.

16
00:00:58,459 --> 00:00:59,446
You edit the file.

17
00:00:59,786 --> 00:01:01,652
git add copies it into the index.

18
00:01:01,992 --> 00:01:05,433
git commit records the
index as a new snapshot.

19
00:01:07,773 --> 00:01:12,394
git restore is 🔴 DANGEROUS for unsaved
work: it copies the index back over your

20
00:01:12,434 --> 00:01:12,689
edit.

21
00:01:13,029 --> 00:01:18,433
And git reset --hard is 🔴 DANGEROUS too: it
moves HEAD back and overwrites the other

22
00:01:18,473 --> 00:01:18,693
two.

23
00:01:20,533 --> 00:01:22,106
A commit is a small object.

24
00:01:22,446 --> 00:01:23,587
It points at a tree.

25
00:01:23,927 --> 00:01:26,225
The tree points at blobs, one per file.

26
00:01:26,565 --> 00:01:29,450
A second commit after one
edit adds one new blob.

27
00:01:29,790 --> 00:01:31,183
Everything else is shared.

28
00:01:33,023 --> 00:01:35,748
An object's ID is computed
from its content.

29
00:01:36,088 --> 00:01:39,379
Identical content gets the
identical ID, on any machine.

30
00:01:39,719 --> 00:01:42,423
Change one byte, and the
ID is a different one.

31
00:01:44,263 --> 00:01:46,583
The lab is a sealed room on your own Mac.

32
00:01:46,923 --> 00:01:51,223
Inside the room is the lab's own
configuration, its clock and its directories.

33
00:01:51,563 --> 00:01:53,624
Outside is everything you care about.

34
00:01:53,964 --> 00:01:56,119
Both lab commands walk into the room.

35
00:01:56,459 --> 00:01:58,760
The room has two doors
with different clocks.

36
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:03,522
And nothing a lab does reaches your real
configuration: no arrow gets there.

37
00:02:05,362 --> 00:02:08,273
Three repositories: yours,
origin, and a teammate's.

38
00:02:08,613 --> 00:02:09,908
Asha pushes a commit.

39
00:02:10,248 --> 00:02:12,459
You fetch: only origin/main moves.

40
00:02:12,799 --> 00:02:15,015
You pull: now your main moves too.

41
00:02:15,355 --> 00:02:15,978
You commit.

42
00:02:16,318 --> 00:02:17,412
You push, and origin catches up.

43
00:02:19,252 --> 00:02:20,531
Four commits on main.

44
00:02:20,871 --> 00:02:23,875
A 🔴 DANGEROUS reset yanks
the label back two commits.

45
00:02:24,215 --> 00:02:26,870
The reflog still lists
where HEAD has been.

46
00:02:27,210 --> 00:02:29,410
One more reset, and the commits are back.

47
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:33,315
A pull request starts with a branch.

48
00:02:33,655 --> 00:02:35,560
You push it and open the pull request.

49
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:37,131
A teammate reviews it.

50
00:02:37,471 --> 00:02:38,356
The checks run.

51
00:02:38,696 --> 00:02:39,623
And it is merged.

52
00:02:41,463 --> 00:02:46,273
A push is the event. GitHub finds the
workflow, starts a job on a fresh runner, runs

53
00:02:46,313 --> 00:02:49,563
the steps in order, and reports
the result on the commit.

54
00:02:51,403 --> 00:03:01,873
A tag has its own shape, and the names
Git writes for itself during a merge are

55
00:03:01,913 --> 00:03:04,401
small hollow chips.

56
00:03:04,741 --> 00:03:07,532
One graph can have several
states. Two names on one commit.

57
00:03:07,872 --> 00:03:12,622
Then a commit: the current branch slides to
it, and the frame follows the picture as

58
00:03:12,662 --> 00:03:13,135
it grows.

59
00:03:13,475 --> 00:03:17,927
A merge can be drawn with as many commits
as the transcript has, under their real

60
00:03:17,967 --> 00:03:22,585
short IDs. Both histories meet at the merge
base, and the merge commit stands to the

61
00:03:22,625 --> 00:03:23,805
right of both parents.

62
00:03:24,145 --> 00:03:27,264
Without a teammate in the story, only
your clone and origin are drawn.

63
00:03:27,304 --> 00:03:28,889
Somebody pushes, and then you fetch.

64
00:03:29,229 --> 00:03:32,799
A pull request can be blocked.
Is it the branch, which is Git?

65
00:03:32,839 --> 00:03:36,818
The review, which is GitHub? Or the
checks, which are GitHub Actions?

66
00:03:37,158 --> 00:03:39,866
The three boxes can be renamed and drawn
in the other order, a long path is fitted,

67
00:03:39,906 --> 00:03:42,416
and the branch can be shown under the
history. You edit, you add, you commit.

68
00:03:44,256 --> 00:03:48,993
A rebase can now carry the transcript's
own commits and their new IDs.

69
00:03:49,033 --> 00:03:54,317
It can stop after one copy with a conflict,
wait, and continue, and at the end

70
00:03:54,357 --> 00:03:56,842
ORIG_HEAD marks where the branch was.

71
00:03:57,182 --> 00:04:04,799
The three-point form takes the commits
that are on client but not on server, and

72
00:04:04,839 --> 00:04:06,809
replays them on main.

73
00:04:07,149 --> 00:04:12,195
The reflog scene takes its own commits,
its own reflog lines and its own rescue.

74
00:04:12,235 --> 00:04:17,408
A commit that only the reflog names is
dashed but not faded, and here a new branch

75
00:04:17,448 --> 00:04:18,361
brings it back.

76
00:04:18,701 --> 00:04:23,662
Any story between repositories is
written as panels in the graph notation.

77
00:04:23,702 --> 00:04:29,271
A push is rejected, git pull --rebase replays
the local commit, and the second push

78
00:04:29,311 --> 00:04:30,150
goes through.

79
00:04:30,490 --> 00:04:34,467
A cherry-pick is drawn as
the three-way merge it is.

80
00:04:34,507 --> 00:04:40,261
The base is the parent of the picked
commit, and the result has one parent.

81
00:04:40,601 --> 00:04:44,813
A revert is the same merge with the roles
turned around: the base is the commit

82
00:04:44,853 --> 00:04:45,512
being undone.

83
00:04:45,852 --> 00:04:49,296
A new file starts in the working tree only.
You add it, then you commit it, and the

84
00:04:49,336 --> 00:04:50,177
first commit appears.

85
00:04:50,517 --> 00:04:57,618
After a commit, a soft reset moves only
HEAD. A mixed reset also rewrites the index.

86
00:04:57,658 --> 00:05:02,548
A hard reset is 🔴 DANGEROUS:
it overwrites your files too.

87
00:05:02,888 --> 00:05:07,604
A box can lack the file, an index entry
can carry a flag, and a restore that

88
00:05:07,644 --> 00:05:09,920
destroys nothing is not drawn in red.

89
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:13,582
The pull request scene takes real commits,
any number of checks and its own words.

90
00:05:13,622 --> 00:05:16,985
A push after the approval dismisses it, and
the squash puts one new commit on main.

91
00:05:17,325 --> 00:05:22,572
A workflow can be skipped by a path filter.
Then no run starts, and a required check

92
00:05:22,612 --> 00:05:23,453
keeps waiting.

93
00:05:23,793 --> 00:05:26,314
Any objects can be drawn as cards: a tag
object, a commit, a tree with a sub-tree

94
00:05:26,354 --> 00:05:28,306
and a gitlink entry, and a blob
that is not in this repository.

95
00:05:28,646 --> 00:05:32,858
The same content can go through two different
functions, and long IDs are set on two

96
00:05:32,898 --> 00:05:33,162
lines.

97
00:05:35,002 --> 00:05:38,288
A commit can carry a note, a
role, a tree ID and a mark.

98
00:05:38,328 --> 00:05:43,217
A range is shaded, fourteen commits are
folded into three dots, and every state has

99
00:05:43,257 --> 00:05:45,177
its own caption and command line.

100
00:05:45,517 --> 00:05:47,380
A held scene can get a new
caption without moving.

101
00:05:47,720 --> 00:05:55,079
Commits without a name, a commit whose ID
is never printed, special refs, two names

102
00:05:55,119 --> 00:06:00,696
that look alike, and a tag object
between a tag and its commit.

103
00:06:01,036 --> 00:06:06,203
Two repositories stand side by side, and
an arrow can cross from a commit of one to

104
00:06:06,243 --> 00:06:07,583
a commit of the other.

105
00:06:07,923 --> 00:06:08,548
Twig can nod when a prediction was right.

106
00:06:08,888 --> 00:06:11,975
And Twig can ask for care without
the alarm of a red label.

107
00:06:12,315 --> 00:06:15,295
A step can name its scene, so the first
graph of this section returns in its second

108
00:06:15,335 --> 00:06:15,513
state.

109
00:06:17,353 --> 00:06:20,809
Bisect halves what is left. Git checks out
the middle, you say good or bad, and the

110
00:06:20,849 --> 00:06:24,053
shaded range shrinks until one commit
remains: four tests instead of fifteen.

111
00:06:24,393 --> 00:06:28,873
Blame tags every line with the
commit that last changed it.

112
00:06:28,913 --> 00:06:34,084
When a reformatting commit is ignored,
two lines point further back.

113
00:06:34,424 --> 00:06:39,255
The todo list of an interactive rebase is
edited: one line becomes a fixup, one is

114
00:06:39,295 --> 00:06:42,285
reworded, one is dropped,
and two commits come out.

115
00:06:42,625 --> 00:06:50,902
A stash entry is two commits, and
refs/stash points at the second one.

116
00:06:50,942 --> 00:06:54,585
After a pop nothing names them.

117
00:06:54,925 --> 00:07:00,237
A lightweight tag points at a commit. An
annotated tag points at a tag object first.

118
00:07:00,577 --> 00:07:05,426
Two working trees have their own HEAD and
their own index, and share one object

119
00:07:05,466 --> 00:07:05,798
store.

120
00:07:06,138 --> 00:07:08,933
A push in parts: the refspec maps a local
name to a remote name, your own Git checks

121
00:07:08,973 --> 00:07:11,700
the fast-forward rule, the server has the
last word, and a lease compares what you

122
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:12,645
expected with what is there.

123
00:07:12,985 --> 00:07:19,625
Fetch with prune removes a remote-tracking
name whose branch no longer exists on the

124
00:07:19,665 --> 00:07:20,182
server.

125
00:07:20,522 --> 00:07:27,481
A shallow clone holds the newest commits.
What lies behind the boundary is drawn as

126
00:07:27,521 --> 00:07:29,758
absent, not as unreachable.

127
00:07:30,098 --> 00:07:34,079
A bundle is one file: a header with refs,
a prerequisite that the receiver must

128
00:07:34,119 --> 00:07:35,351
already have, and a pack.

129
00:07:35,691 --> 00:07:41,830
A subtree is another project's history
merged into a directory of this one.

130
00:07:42,170 --> 00:07:46,957
With Git LFS a small pointer file goes
into Git and the bytes go into a store of

131
00:07:46,997 --> 00:07:51,905
their own. The clean filter works on the
way in, the smudge filter on the way out.

132
00:07:52,245 --> 00:07:55,086
Hooks are gates on a line. Two stand on
your machine, three on the server, and each

133
00:07:55,126 --> 00:07:55,989
one says what it can stop.

134
00:07:56,329 --> 00:07:58,961
Filters and line-ending rules change the
form of the content between the working

135
00:07:59,001 --> 00:07:59,595
tree and the index.

136
00:07:59,935 --> 00:08:07,839
Loose objects become a pack: one base,
and deltas that say how to rebuild the

137
00:08:07,879 --> 00:08:08,562
others.

138
00:08:08,902 --> 00:08:11,968
A few numbers can be shown
as bars, one at a time.

139
00:08:12,308 --> 00:08:14,734
The recovery ladder: reachable, only
in the reflog, unreachable, pruned.

140
00:08:14,774 --> 00:08:16,413
Each rung says what still
brings the commit back.

141
00:08:16,753 --> 00:08:25,033
A decision tree takes its own questions
and its own leaves, and can show the way

142
00:08:25,073 --> 00:08:26,593
that was taken.

143
00:08:26,933 --> 00:08:31,734
A table is walked row by row: base, ours,
theirs, and the result for each file.

144
00:08:32,074 --> 00:08:37,113
A few statements can stand as cards, arrive
one by one, and then be ticked, ringed

145
00:08:37,153 --> 00:08:37,732
or locked.

146
00:08:39,572 --> 00:08:42,751
A pull request can be approved and
green and still be blocked by a rule.

147
00:08:43,091 --> 00:08:46,223
On an object card a bar can mark
the lines that a signature covers.

148
00:08:46,563 --> 00:08:48,610
Every label in the platform
pictures comes from the script.

149
00:08:48,650 --> 00:08:50,414
Here layers of rules add
up, and one of them fails.

150
00:08:50,754 --> 00:08:56,034
One push meets the rules one after the other.
A bypass lets it pass a rule; the next

151
00:08:56,074 --> 00:08:56,921
rule stops it.

152
00:08:57,261 --> 00:09:10,715
For each changed path the matching
lines light up, and the last one wins.

153
00:09:11,055 --> 00:09:13,733
Three merge methods side by side,
with what lands on the base branch.

154
00:09:14,073 --> 00:09:16,897
A fork network: two sets of
refs over one object store.

155
00:09:17,237 --> 00:09:23,796
An authentication path is a sequence between
actors, and the place where it fails is

156
00:09:23,836 --> 00:09:24,896
marked in red.

157
00:09:25,236 --> 00:09:34,223
One workflow run in detail: the event and
the commit it runs on, jobs that need

158
00:09:34,263 --> 00:09:42,679
other jobs, a matrix, the token's
permissions, a cache and an artifact, an

159
00:09:42,719 --> 00:09:52,277
environment that waits for a reviewer, and
an older run cancelled by its concurrency

160
00:09:52,317 --> 00:09:52,963
group.

161
00:09:53,303 --> 00:09:58,119
A trusted and an untrusted side, and
what may cross the line between them.

162
00:09:58,459 --> 00:10:05,995
A mutable tag is moved to another commit.
A pin by commit ID stays where it was.

163
00:10:06,335 --> 00:10:07,891
A token exchange instead
of a stored secret.

164
00:10:08,231 --> 00:10:10,126
The response to a leaked secret
is a sequence in a fixed order.

165
00:10:10,466 --> 00:10:14,730
And a rewritten history can come back from
a stale clone that pushes its old commits

166
00:10:14,770 --> 00:10:15,037
again.

167
00:10:16,877 --> 00:10:20,895
Scenes are triggered by tags. One
line in the script picks the scene.

168
00:10:21,235 --> 00:10:24,812
Steps follow the narration. Each
paragraph plays the next step.

169
00:10:25,152 --> 00:10:30,411
Everything else animates by itself.
Terminals type, tables and lists reveal.
